  "id": "son_de_artesa",
  "name": "Son de artesa (Costa Chica platform-dance son)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "The son de artesa is the Afro-Mexican fandango of the Costa Chica of Guerrero and Oaxaca, kept above all in San Nicolás Tolentino and El Ciruelo, in which couples dance zapateado on the artesa, a long platform hollowed from a single tree trunk and carved with an animal's head, so that the dancers' feet sound as the ensemble's drum. A violin carries the melody over a guitar and a struck wooden box, under sung coplas, and INAH's Testimonio Musical de México series documented it. A generic percussion card stands in for the cajón, the struck wooden box, and the stomp card for the artesa.",
  "recipe": "classic modern son de artesa, functional Song, Mexican branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment, single lead single track no stacking. fiddle, parlor acoustic guitar, credited auxiliary percussion, stomp boot hollow platform foot stomp. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
